A||N||9||V8Dreamer||Prepping a stored car/engine before starting....||||1||
Z||000000||V8Dreamer||07-17-2001||10:42 AM||||Well, a guy I work with knows I love Fieros and has an 86 GT himself, however it actually used to be his wife's daily driver and he hates Fieros.  Anyway, his brother was supposed to pick it up yesterday and didn't show, so now he's GIVING it to me...that's right, GIVING!  It's only got about 60k on it.....needs a new fascia, fender, quarter windows and pass side glass, but IT'S FREE!  Now, my question is:  What should I do to the engine before starting it.....I once read something about pulling the plugs and squiring some ATF into the cylinders and then cranking the engine over by hand...any suggestions? I just don't wanna ruin a good running engine.<P>Sorry this got to be so windy,<BR>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||07-17-2001||10:54 AM||||Drain and refill all the fluids.  Then pull the plugs and squirt some oil in the cylinders.  I've heard of people using ATF.  Personally, I'd use Marvel Mystery Oil, or a light machine oil.  Ideally, pulling the distributor and spinning up the oil pump to get pressure would be a grand idea, but turning the engine over by hand to circulate the oil you squirted in the cylinders will definately help.  I'd then pull the coil wire and crank the engine till oil pressure comes up.  Then reconnect the coil and start the engine.<P>After you've run the engine a while - maybe driven it a bit, change the oil again.  I'd change it either the same day, or at least within a week.  That'll help clean out anything that got flushed into the oil when the engine was started again.<P>Good luck, and congrats on the GT!<p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 07-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steve Normington||07-17-2001||11:00 AM||||IMHO, ATF sounds like a really bad idea. It has very little lubricating properties and it will wash off any residual oil. The big question is how long has the car been stored.<BR>If its a few months, then new coolant, oil, and rubber (belts and hoses) should be enough. You might also need to replace the battery. Once that is done, pull the fuel pump fuse and crank the engine until you get oil pressure.<BR>If it is years, then you start getting into the tougher stuff (varnish in the fuel system, corrosion in the cylinders, etc.) ||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||07-17-2001||12:18 PM||||Ive always added a quart of ATF fluid to oil in crankcase when I got a car. ran it normally for a week or so, then drained and changed oil and filter. Cleans out oil passages and gook all thru it. I never had a problem with the engines because of it. ATF does have some pretty potent solvents, thats why i used it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Dave Gunsul||07-17-2001||01:22 PM||||Steve has asked the million dollar question.<BR>How long has it been stored? This will make all the difference in what you need to do.<P>Pulling the distributer and spinning the oil pump with a drill would be the best idea<BR>after changing the fluids though.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000005||V8Dreamer||07-17-2001||09:45 PM||||The answer to the million dollar question is 2 YEARS......any more advice?<P>Thanks,<BR>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||07-17-2001||11:46 PM||||A squirt of ATF in the cylinders is the accepted way of getting a stored engine ready to run. I don't care if it sounds like a bad idea, it works. It dissolves junk and burns away in the first few seconds of running.<P>Anyway, take the rear valve cover off, remove the distributor and turn the oil pump with a drill until you see oil coming up through ALL SIX pushrods on that side. You really should make sure oil comes up through all twelve pushrods, but the front valve cover is such a PITA I don't think it's worth it. WARNING: it may take 10 minutes of turning the pump with a drill to get oil to the pushrods.<BR>Once you've primed the oil passages, remove all the spark plugs and put a couple of teaspoons of ATF in each cylinder. Remove the RR wheel and wheel house, put a socket on the balancer bolt and turn the crank about two dozen revolutions with the plugs out.<BR>Put the plugs back in and fire it up!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Dave Gunsul||07-18-2001||01:19 AM||||That's good advice from Will above BUT don't<BR>forget about the fuel!<BR>After 2 years it's probably not able to even<BR>run the lawn mower.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I know that you're not going to want to hear this but it wouldn't be a bad idea to flush the tank out and put fresh gas in there.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||14
Z||000008||sqoach||07-18-2001||01:48 AM||||You could always part it out if it's too much trouble. I need a front a rear fascia  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forumsig.JPG">      <BR>84 SE (FOR SALE)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)(take it off my hands for $50)<BR>86 SC (FOR SALE sometime in this summer)<BR>86 SE V6 (new project)||||reg||1
Z||000009||V8Dreamer||07-18-2001||08:28 AM||||No, it's not too much trouble.....plus, I can't see sacraficing a GT like this man, it needs some TLC, but other than that it's way too complete to tear down.  Plus, the front fascia is a little beat up anyway.<P>Thanks for the advice guys,<BR>Ryan||||reg||1
